 "interpretation": [
  "Sentences are split and tokenised by atact.lexicon. In the variant without function words a matched term is dropped when every token of it is in the stop-word list; V, A and Z are then recomputed from the remaining terms, and Z = 0 gives (0, 0) with the unmatched flag, as in the main variant.",
  "The gradient of S2.6 is numpy.gradient(X, 1/N), its magnitude hypot(d/dx, d/dy), maximised over all grid points, edges included. Physical slope = (height scale) |grad H| / L with height scale D / (2 s_max) for the common exports and D / (max H - min H) for min-max; angle = atan(slope) in degrees.",
  "P-T1 compares the exported min-max image z / D with (T - min T) / (max T - min T), T = T_gamma(F), written out separately, elementwise. P-T3 uses G = max |grad F| on the same grid and is checked only for gamma > 1 (sigma_eps is defined there). P-T4 is judged per text when at least two of its sentences are unmatched in the variant without function words: their (V, A) must be (0, 0), their parameters those of (0, 0), and their tiles equal on every grid and export; otherwise it is reported as not applicable. All texts share the seed and the grids.",
  "Before any tile is computed, the sentence counts and Z of every text and variant are compared with 'expected_coverage' of the conditions; a difference stops the run. A prediction that fails does not stop the run; it is reported as it comes out. The real run refuses to start when the tracked inputs differ from the last commit.",
  "edge_argmax records whether the grid point of the largest slope lies in the first or last row or column; f is in general not an integer here, so the field jumps at the domain edge (S1.6), and the one-sided differences at the edge do not see the jump.",
  "In the figure, unmatched sentences (Z = 0) are ringed, and labels of coincident points are merged.",
  "Grey levels of the figure: z / D + 1/2 for the common exports (so that z = 0 is mid grey and |z| <= D/2 under ampsum and s <= s_max), z / D for min-max; both on [0, 1] with the grey colour map, no shading, origin lower."
 ],
